@startuml scdawg-factor-extensions
' ============================================================================
' scdawg-factor-extensions.puml — the two edge families of ONE prime subword P.
' Right extensions APPEND a character (P·σ, the CDAWG's native direction);
' left extensions PREPEND a character (σ·P, the symmetric edges the SCDAWG adds).
' Together they give bidirectional growth of a matched factor.
' Source: §Visual Representation of 04-scdawg.md.
' Color key (house palette + scdawg accents):
'   teal   #B2DFDB  the focus factor P (where you currently are)
'   green  #C8E6C9  right-extension targets (append — CDAWG direction)
'   blue   #BBDEFB  left-extension targets  (prepend — symmetric SCDAWG edges)
' ============================================================================
skinparam defaultFontSize 14
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam defaultFontName "DejaVu Sans"
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #37474F
  FontColor #212121
  BorderThickness 1.3
}

title Left vs right extensions of a prime subword **P**\nprepend σ·P (left, blue) · P · append P·σ (right, green)

left to right direction

rectangle "σ₁·P" as L1 #BBDEFB
rectangle "σ₂·P" as L2 #BBDEFB
rectangle "**P**\n(current factor)" as P #B2DFDB
rectangle "P·σ₁" as R1 #C8E6C9
rectangle "P·σ₂" as R2 #C8E6C9

P -left-> L1 : "prepend σ₁\n(left ext ←)"
P -left-> L2 : "prepend σ₂\n(left ext ←)"
P -right-> R1 : "append σ₁\n(right ext →)"
P -right-> R2 : "append σ₂\n(right ext →)"

note bottom of P
  Left extension P → σ·P and right extension P → P·σ each
  cost O(|label|). "cab" is the **left** ext of "ab" by 'c';
  "abc" is the **right** ext — different strings, not a reversal.
end note
@enduml
